survived.mechanism:
GenericRoomIdsEngine._build_per_room_phases (queue/dispatch_engines.py:266-281) emits ONE phase per room, each with `"resolved_rooms": [room]` and `payload = {rooms_field: [segment_id]}`. jobs/phase_runner.py:1019-1027 then calls `_resolve_live_dispatch_payload(payload=job.get("payload"), resolved_rooms=list(job.get("resolved_rooms", [])))` — i.e. with a ONE-element resolved_rooms — and dispatches whatever comes back with no further inspection. Inside `_resolve_live_dispatch_payload` the loop builds `new_segments` from slug->live-id, appending unresolved slugs to `dropped`; then:

    if dropped:
        _LOGGER.warning("dispatch: %d target room(s) not on the current map for %s, skipped: %s", ...)
    if not new_segments:
        _LOGGER.warning("dispatch: no target rooms resolved live for %s; dispatching stored ids", ...)
        return payload            # <-- the STALE stored id goes to the device
    return {**payload, rooms_field: new_segments}

In the ATOMIC batch path the two branches are distinct: one target dropped out of five still leaves `new_segments` non-empty, so the dropped room really is skipped, exactly as the docstring promises ("A target whose slug is absent from the current map is skipped (it can't be targeted) rather than cleaned under a stale id"). Under strict_order the phase has exactly ONE target, so `dropped` non-empty ALWAYS implies `new_segments == []`, and the 'live source unavailable' fallback fires for what is actually a resolved-and-rejected target. Roborock is precisely the brand where this matters: `dispatch.resolve_live_ids_by_slug: True` exists (adapters/roborock/adapter.py:353) because "Segment ids RENUMBER on re-segment (identity = name slug)" — so the stored id now points at a DIFFERENT room. phase_runner.py:1028-1030 documents the opposite belief ("an empty segment list = the next room was skipped, e.g. a slug that didn't resolve to a live id") — a comment describing behaviour the code does not have.

_resolve_live_dispatch_payload builds `slug_to_live_id` from a fresh get_maps, then walks the targets:
```
for room in resolved_rooms:
    slug = ...
    live_id = slug_to_live_id.get(slug)
    if live_id is not None: new_segments.append(live_id)
    else: dropped.append(...)
...
if not new_segments:
    _LOGGER.warning("dispatch: no target rooms resolved live for %s; dispatching stored ids", ...)
    return payload            # <-- the STORED, stale ids go on the wire
```
The docstring two screens above promises the opposite: "A target whose slug is absent from the current map is skipped (it can't be targeted) rather than cleaned under a stale id." That holds for a PARTIAL miss (those rooms are dropped) and is inverted for a TOTAL miss. Reached when every stored slug is absent from the live map while the live source itself is healthy — i.e. exactly a full re-segment / bulk rename in the Roborock app, which is the scenario the adapter itself calls out at adapters/roborock/adapter.py:348 ("Segment ids RENUMBER on re-segment (identity = name slug) ... A stored id could otherwise clean the wrong room after a map edit"). Roborock reuses low segment ids (16,17,18…), so the stale ids resolve to different physical rooms on the new segmentation. Note the branch is NOT the same as the `if not slug_to_live_id` branch above it — that one is the defensible "live source unavailable" case; this one fires with a fully-populated live map that simply contains none of the stored names.

```
_opts = [str(o).strip().lower() for o in _sel_state.attributes.get("options") or []] if _sel_state is not None else []
if _opts and "off" not in _opts:
    for _cand in rank:
        if _cand in _opts:
            canonical_value = _cand
            break
value_map = entry.get("value_map") or {}
wire_value = value_map.get(canonical_value, canonical_value)
```
The select's ORIGINAL option strings are discarded — only the lowercased copies survive. A brand whose mop select exposes `["Off", "Low", "High"]` therefore satisfies `"off" in _opts`, skips the fallback, and sends `option: "off"`, which select.select_option rejects as not a valid option; the exception is swallowed by the handler at 442 and the device keeps its previous (possibly high) water. Second, smaller category error: `_cand` is drawn from `rank` (canonical vocabulary) and tested against `_opts` (WIRE vocabulary), so on any entry with a non-identity `value_map` the loop can never match and the fallback is dead code; line 432 then applies value_map to a value that may already be a wire option. Latent today — Roborock's mop_intensity options are lowercase and 1:1 with the canonical rank (adapters/roborock/adapter.py:137, "canonical off/low/medium/high map 1:1 onto select.<obj>_mop_intensity's options (no value_map)").
